The identical anti-pattern was found and fixed elsewhere the same day.
`std/runtime:parse.source` handed interpreted source to the COMPILER's file
parser under an invented filename ("runtime_eval.kz"), and because that parser
picks host-vs-pure from the extension (src/parser.zig:707, defaulting to
host-embedded for anything not ending `.k`), the invented name silently
answered a question the source does not have. Measured there: `greet(...)`
failed and `~greet(...)` ran green — the exact inverse of the shipped rule. The
fix carried no design content: the parse entry point now takes no file name at
all, so there is no parameter left to answer by accident.

Lars, on being shown that: "There is no 'dialect' for interpreted files, it's
always PURE KORU." That ruling is about INTERPRETER source, which a test body
is not — a test body is nested Koru inside a real file that has a real dialect.
So it does not settle this one by itself. What it does settle is the shape of
the mistake: a synthetic filename is not a neutral placeholder, it is a silent
answer.

One more thing that points the same way, and is inside this file rather than
by analogy: `koru_std/testing.kz:36` ALREADY prefers the source's real file
when it has one, and only falls back to "__test__.kz". Line 280 hardcodes the
fallback unconditionally. Two lines in one file disagree about whether the real
filename matters, which makes it likelier that one of them is simply a bug
rather than that the language wants a mode declaration here.

Still unresolved and still a human call: whether a test body should inherit the
entry file's dialect (b), or declare its own mode (the TODO's second reading).
